WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:03.060
SPEAKER: Welcome to Google Cloud
Drawing Board, where we doodle

00:00:03.060 --> 00:00:04.490
our way through the Cloud.

00:00:04.490 --> 00:00:07.980
Today's topic--
What is Cloud CDN?

00:00:07.980 --> 00:00:09.750
This video is divided
into chapters.

00:00:09.750 --> 00:00:11.280
Watch the full
video or skip ahead

00:00:11.280 --> 00:00:13.780
to a new section of your choice.

00:00:13.780 --> 00:00:16.440
Whatever your app or
website is, chances

00:00:16.440 --> 00:00:18.600
are that your users
are distributed

00:00:18.600 --> 00:00:20.550
across different
locations and are not

00:00:20.550 --> 00:00:23.230
necessarily close to
where your servers are.

00:00:23.230 --> 00:00:26.400
This means the requests travel
across the public internet

00:00:26.400 --> 00:00:30.180
through long distances, leading
to inconsistent and frustrating

00:00:30.180 --> 00:00:31.710
user experiences.

00:00:31.710 --> 00:00:34.345
That's where Cloud CDN comes in.

00:00:34.345 --> 00:00:36.840
Cloud CDN is a content
delivery network

00:00:36.840 --> 00:00:39.330
that accelerates your
web and video content

00:00:39.330 --> 00:00:42.210
delivery by using
Google's Global Edge

00:00:42.210 --> 00:00:46.750
Network to bring content as
close to the user as possible.

00:00:46.750 --> 00:00:49.650
As a result, the
latency, cost, and load

00:00:49.650 --> 00:00:52.830
on your backend servers is
reduced, making it easier

00:00:52.830 --> 00:00:55.270
to scale to millions of users.

00:00:55.270 --> 00:00:57.930
Let's consider an app to
understand how this works.

00:00:57.930 --> 00:01:01.230
When a user makes a request
to your website or app,

00:01:01.230 --> 00:01:04.500
the request is routed to the
closest Google Edge Node.

00:01:04.500 --> 00:01:09.030
We have all 120 of these for
fast and reliable traffic flow.

00:01:09.030 --> 00:01:12.090
Then, to the global
HTTP(S) load balancer,

00:01:12.090 --> 00:01:14.430
to the backend, or the origin.

00:01:14.430 --> 00:01:16.940
From there, with
Cloud CDN enabled,

00:01:16.940 --> 00:01:19.750
we'll serve the content
directly from cache.

00:01:19.750 --> 00:01:23.370
Cache is nothing but a group of
servers that store and manage

00:01:23.370 --> 00:01:26.880
cacheable content so that
future requests for that content

00:01:26.880 --> 00:01:28.790
can be solved faster.

00:01:28.790 --> 00:01:31.680
The cached content is a
copy of cachable content

00:01:31.680 --> 00:01:34.050
that is stored on
your origin servers,

00:01:34.050 --> 00:01:38.370
typically including web assets
like JavaScript, CSS, images,

00:01:38.370 --> 00:01:39.530
and videos.

00:01:39.530 --> 00:01:42.690
Cloud CDN can automatically
cache this content

00:01:42.690 --> 00:01:45.930
with just a single checkbox,
using the recommended cache

00:01:45.930 --> 00:01:48.420
modes to cache all
static content.

00:01:48.420 --> 00:01:51.330
If you need more control, you
can direct Cloud seeding CDN

00:01:51.330 --> 00:01:54.180
by setting HTTP headers
on your responses.

00:01:54.180 --> 00:01:57.180
And you can also force
all content to be cached.

00:01:57.180 --> 00:01:59.850
Just know that this ignores
the private, no-store,

00:01:59.850 --> 00:02:03.270
or no-cache directives in
cache control response headers.

00:02:03.270 --> 00:02:05.940
When the request is
received by Cloud CDN,

00:02:05.940 --> 00:02:09.440
it looks for the cached content
using the cache key, which

00:02:09.440 --> 00:02:10.949
is typically the URI.

00:02:10.949 --> 00:02:12.990
But you can customize
this cache key

00:02:12.990 --> 00:02:16.200
to remove protocol
posts or query strings.

00:02:16.200 --> 00:02:18.750
For the user's request,
if a cached response

00:02:18.750 --> 00:02:21.510
is found in Cloud CDN
cache, the response

00:02:21.510 --> 00:02:24.520
is retrieved from cache
and sent to the user.

00:02:24.520 --> 00:02:26.700
This is called a cache hit.

00:02:26.700 --> 00:02:29.340
When a cache hit
occurs, Cloud CDN

00:02:29.340 --> 00:02:31.470
looks at the content
byte's cache key

00:02:31.470 --> 00:02:33.600
and responds
directly to the user,

00:02:33.600 --> 00:02:36.510
shortening the round trip time
and saving the origin server

00:02:36.510 --> 00:02:38.610
from having to
process the request.

00:02:38.610 --> 00:02:41.630
The first time that a piece
of content is requested,

00:02:41.630 --> 00:02:44.710
the Cloud CDN can't fulfill
the request from the cache

00:02:44.710 --> 00:02:46.990
because it doesn't
have it there.

00:02:46.990 --> 00:02:49.230
This is called a cache miss.

00:02:49.230 --> 00:02:51.660
When a cache miss
occurs, the Cloud CDN

00:02:51.660 --> 00:02:54.900
might attempt to get the
content from the nearby cache.

00:02:54.900 --> 00:02:56.880
If the nearby cache
has the content,

00:02:56.880 --> 00:03:00.750
it sends it to the first cache
by using cache-to-cache fill.

00:03:00.750 --> 00:03:02.460
Otherwise, it just
sends the request

00:03:02.460 --> 00:03:03.720
through the origin server.

00:03:03.720 --> 00:03:06.510
The maximum lifetime
of the object in cache

00:03:06.510 --> 00:03:10.560
is defined by the TTLs,
or time to live values,

00:03:10.560 --> 00:03:13.980
set by the cache directives
from each a HTTP response,

00:03:13.980 --> 00:03:15.150
or cache modes.

00:03:15.150 --> 00:03:18.990
When the TTL has expired, the
content is evicted from catch.

00:03:18.990 --> 00:03:21.240
Now, how do you use Cloud CDN?

00:03:21.240 --> 00:03:24.600
You can set up Cloud CBN through
GCloud Command Line interface,

00:03:24.600 --> 00:03:27.030
Could Consol, or the APIs.

00:03:27.030 --> 00:03:31.140
Since Cloud CBN leverages Google
Cloud global external HTTP(S)

00:03:31.140 --> 00:03:33.450
load balancers to
provide routing, health

00:03:33.450 --> 00:03:35.640
checking, and
Anycast support, it

00:03:35.640 --> 00:03:39.180
can be enabled by easily
checking a box while setting up

00:03:39.180 --> 00:03:41.130
the backends or the origins.

00:03:41.130 --> 00:03:45.270
Cloud CDN makes it even easier
to solve web and media content

00:03:45.270 --> 00:03:47.010
using Google Cloud Storage.

00:03:47.010 --> 00:03:50.700
You just upload your content to
a bucket, set up load balancer,

00:03:50.700 --> 00:03:52.010
and enable caching.

00:03:52.010 --> 00:03:54.810
And if you are like
many of our customers, ,

00:03:54.810 --> 00:03:58.620
you probably have services that
are on-prem or in other clouds.

00:03:58.620 --> 00:04:01.200
To enable these
hybrid architectures,

00:04:01.200 --> 00:04:04.030
Cloud CDN and HTTP(S)
load balancing

00:04:04.030 --> 00:04:06.690
also support external backends.

00:04:06.690 --> 00:04:08.910
Let's get into the
underlying details now.

00:04:08.910 --> 00:04:14.010
Global Anycast IPs provide
single IP for global reach.

00:04:14.010 --> 00:04:17.730
It allows Google Cloud to
round users to the nearest edge

00:04:17.730 --> 00:04:21.570
cache automatically and avoid
relying on the DNS propagation

00:04:21.570 --> 00:04:24.360
delays that can
impact availability.

00:04:24.360 --> 00:04:29.130
It also supports HTTP end-to-end
and quick protocol from cache

00:04:29.130 --> 00:04:29.970
to cache.

00:04:29.970 --> 00:04:32.550
QUIC is a
multiplex-to-stream transport

00:04:32.550 --> 00:04:35.730
over UDP, which
reduces latency, making

00:04:35.730 --> 00:04:38.760
it ideal for lossy
mobile networks.

00:04:38.760 --> 00:04:40.770
That brings me to security.

00:04:40.770 --> 00:04:45.210
Data is encrypted at rest and in
transit from Google Cloud load

00:04:45.210 --> 00:04:48.330
balancing to backend, supporting
an end-to-end encrypted

00:04:48.330 --> 00:04:49.440
experience.

00:04:49.440 --> 00:04:52.410
You can programmatically
sign URLs and cookies

00:04:52.410 --> 00:04:56.550
to limit video segment access
to all authorized users only.

00:04:56.550 --> 00:04:59.610
The signature is
validated at the CDN Edge

00:04:59.610 --> 00:05:01.830
and the request that
should not get through

00:05:01.830 --> 00:05:03.360
is blocked right there.

00:05:03.360 --> 00:05:06.060
On a broader level,
you can enable SSL

00:05:06.060 --> 00:05:09.380
for free using
Google managed certs.

00:05:09.380 --> 00:05:12.900
Now, to summarize, use Cloud
CDN to improve performance

00:05:12.900 --> 00:05:15.540
and reduce serving costs
for regularly accessed

00:05:15.540 --> 00:05:18.480
content by automatically
caching static content

00:05:18.480 --> 00:05:19.940
with a click of a button.

00:05:19.940 --> 00:05:22.050
Want to learn more
about Cloud CDN?

00:05:22.050 --> 00:05:25.150
Check out cloud.google.com/cdn.

